.th SLUP I "1 July 77"
.sh NAME
slup \*- source librarian and update program
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd slup
library -[function][subswitch][/modname]... file1 file2 ... filen
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Slup
is an improved
librarian or archiver with the ability to make logged modifications
to source files that are stored in the library.
.s3
When
.it slup
is called without arguments it will print out
a very brief summary of the available commands.
.s3
When given only a library name,
.it slup
tests the validity of
the named file to make sure that it is indeed a source library.
.s3
If
.it slup
is called with more than one argument then the following
list of switches applies.
If no switch is supplied then the default is
"\fB-i\fR".
.s3
Most switches may be followed by `v' for verbose.
The only exception is "\fI-n\fR".
This usually means that the name of the module gets typed
before the operation is attempted.
In the case of the "\fB-l\fR" switch, it actually gives
a much more informative output.
.s3
There is a strange thing called groups built into this librarian.
Any modules may be optionally included in up to 16 named
groups.
If this is done it is possible to extract one or more groups
by name.
This allows very simple grouping of files, still
allowing some files to be common to a number of packages.
The group functions are supposed to have group specifiers
instead of modset specifers.
.s3
	-switches[/groupname]...
.s3
A group-name is up to 10 characters long.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-n	Create a new library.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-i	Requires a following list of file names which are inserted
into the library.
If a module of the same name is already in the library,
.it slup
is
aborted.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-r	Identical in operation to "\fB-i\fR" except that modules
already contained in the library may be replaced.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-l	List the names of all of the modules contained in the
library.
If a list of names is supplied, then only they are listed.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-x[c]
.br
Extract all of the modules in the library.
If a list of names is supplied then only those are extracted.
.sp
The `\fIc\fR' sub-switch
forces the extraction of common files if no module names
are specified.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\&-xo	Used to extract the original file
without any modifications applied at all.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\&-xu[/modnames]...
.br
Used to produce a file which when
applied to
.it slup
as update directives would make the
modifications necessary to produce the current library.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-p[a][c][d][y][?]
.br
Slightly similar to `\fI-x\fR' in that this function
extracts a module from the library.
The difference is that no source file is produced.
Instead, a listing is produced on the printer describing
the file in the library.
The sub-switches are used to select different printing options.
`\fIa\fR' select all printing options; i.e. everything
gets printed that I can think of.
`\fIc\fR' allows included common files to be listed.
`\fId\fR' prints any deleted lines.
`\fIy\fR' prints any lines contained in yanked modification sets.
`\fI?\fR' will be a temporary measure whilst we think of
a good switch to allow directives be printed.
The default if no sub-switches are used is: "\fId?\fR".
.s3
.lp +6 5
-d	Delete the named modules from the library.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-du[/modname]...
.br
Delete named modifications from each of the
named source files in the library.
If no modification names are specified, then all modification
sets will be deleted.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-u[p]
.lp +6 5
-t[p]
.br
Update the modules in the library.
The update directives are taken from standard input
(i.e. at the terminal).
For the format and description of the directives to update
the source files see below.
If the `\fIp\fR' sub-switch is used then a report will be
produced describing the effect of the update run.
The `\fIt\fR' (test) function is different from update in
the following respects:
the library itself is not altered in any way;
the temporary updated modules are always extracted
immediately after the update.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-c	Will flag the specified files as being common files.
About all that this means is that you may have to use
"\fI-xc\fR" when you want to extract them.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-g/groupname...
.br
The modules in the specified groups will be involved in
some other function to be specifed with another
function switch.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-gi/groupname...
.br
Add the named modules to the specified groups.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-gd/groupname...
.br
Delete the specified group-names from the group directory.
.s3
.lp +6 5
-gn/groupname...
.br
Add the specified group-names to the group directory.
.i0
.dt
.s3
UPDATE DIRECTIVES
.br
.s3
The update directives are structured somewhat like editor
commands.
They all begin with an \fIescape\fR character which is by
default `*', but may be changed.
If it is desired to enter a text line beginning with the
escape character, then the escape character may itself be
escaped by preceding it with a reverse-slant (`\\').
All modification runs on any source module must be
given a unique name.
This must be different from all modification set names
already associated with that particular source module.
The modification set name may have up to 10 characters.
.s3
Some of the update directives require a line specifier.
This is formatted thus:
.br
	[modsetname.]linenumber
.br
The null modsetname represents the original source file.
The format of the line specifier will now be: ".[linenumber]".
If no modsetname or `.' is given then a default name
is used.
This is set with the "\fI*modname\fR" directive and
is initally the null modsetname.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*p [char]
.lp +5 5
*prefix [char]
.br
Change the current escape character to "char".
.s3
.lp +5 5
*dk filename
.lp +5 5
*deck filename
.br
Set \fIslup\fR up to update the named source module.
If another source module was just being updated, the updated
module is placed in safe keeping for replacing in the library.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*id modset
.lp +5 5
*ident modset
.br
Name to be used for this modification set.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*m modset
.lp +5 5
*modname modset
.br
A name to be used as the default name in any following
source file line address specifiers.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*a linespec
.lp +5 5
*after linespec
.lp +5 5
*i linespec
.lp +5 5
*insert linespec
.br
Specify a position in the file to insert some text.
Any text following this directive is inserted after the
specified line.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*b linespec
.lp +5 5
*before linespec
.br
Has a similar function to "\fI*after\fR" but allows text
to be inserted before a line instead of after.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*d linespec1[,linespec2]
.lp +5 5
*delete linespec1[,linespec2]
.br
Deletes all text between the two line-specifiers.
"Linespec1" must be less than "linespec2" in value.
If only one line-specifier is supplied then only that line will
be deleted.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*r linespec1[,linespec2]
.lp +5 5
*restore linespec1[,linespec2]
.br
Restores all previously deleted text between the two
line-specifiers.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*c filename
.lp +5 5
*call filename
.br
When the file is extracted, then the specified module in the
library is included at this point.
The included module will often be a "common" file.
This is simply a line in the text; not a directive.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*y modsetname
.lp +5 5
*yank modsetname
.br
Will yank the specified modification set from this file.
When a modification set is yanked then anything that that
modification set did will be ignored.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*u modsetname
.lp +5 5
*unyank modsetname
.br
Unyanks the specified modification set from this file.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*e
.lp +5 5
*exit
.br
Exit from this update run.
Mainly supplied so that MPL may be used in update mode within
command files.
.s3
.lp +5 5
** ....
.lp +5 5
*/ ....
.br
Allows comments to be inserted with the update directives.
These do not come out on the resultant file.
In the case with the asterisk (**), the comment gets saved in
the library and will come out on listings.
.i0
.dt
.sh FILES
/tmp/slup#
.sh "SEE ALSO"
macro(I), object(V), lpr(I)
.sh AUTHOR
Craig McGregor (University of New South Wales)
.sh BUGS
Many and varied.
.br
Groups and non-source library features are not fully implemented.
Similarly common files.
